and speaking of the franchise tag the  lions have a candidate  in receiver kenny galladay dan campbell  new head coach addressing the media  today talking about  the possibility of tagging kenny  gallaudet and dusting off an old take  about whether or not it's actually a  good thing to be tagged here's campbell  well i would love to be franchised that  would be nice because that's  it's a pretty good it's a pretty good  little chunk right now but  i don't want to get that far into it i  really don't michael i'm sorry  uh look all these things will be coming  down the road you know before the 17th  obviously  and uh you know look we've we've had  dialogue we've talked back and forth  brad and i have talked about this i mean  we've gone every scenario you could go  down  believe it or not just on everybody  dan campbell said recently he doesn't  care if people view him as a meat head  and that's good  that's very good that he doesn't care  because as archie bunker once said to  his son-in-law dead from the neck up  meat head come on it's not a good thing  to be franchise tagged yeah it's a nice  payday for one year but what it does  and when a head coach says something  like this fans in media get twisted up  about whether or not it really is a good  thing  it's not a good thing it is an  impediment on the ability of the player  to get maximum value on the open market  and to get generational wealth  through a long-term deal that pays out  in fully guaranteed increments  a hell of a lot more than what that  franchise tag is going to be  so the team uses it not as a favor to  the player  the team uses it as a favor to itself to  limit what the guy makes  to keep him from hitting the open market  and then controlling his rights for  another year meanwhile he's the one  carrying the injury risk  he's the one bearing the risk that he's  not going to be as attractive to another  team a year from now  as he is now he's got to get through 16  more games  and stay at the level he's at to get the  payday next year  shireen that he should have gotten this  year and it's all gonna fall just right  for him to get that payday next year  that he should have gotten this year so  please coach campbell don't tell us  don't insult  our intelligence i mean i've got limited  intelligence so when it's insulted i get  pissed  don't try to tell me it's a good thing  for a player to be franchise tag  i thought we crossed that bridge 25  years ago shireen okay let's back up one  second to aaron jones you said you would  sign the tag immediately if it's 11  does it depend on the player and the  position are you  like that across the board or is that  just for this year on aaron jones  it's running back and if i'm aaron jones  and they're going to keep me from the  market i'll take i'll take my eight  figures because of the position because  here's the thing  and this is why i don't know why the  packers would  use the franchise tag on him this was  explained to me  last year in the context of one of the  running backs who signed a long-term  contract with his current team and i  believe  this analysis the situation running  backs  only get big money long-term deals from  the team that they became  a star with you hit the market you're  melvin gordon at 8 million a year  that's why if i'm aaron jones i'm saying  hey if you're going to pay me 11.1  million this year  i'll take it because you may wake up one  day and realize  i i shouldn't be tagged so in his unique  situation  yeah i guess you could say it's not bad  because he's getting a payday that  he's not gonna get an average contract  to that amount on the open market i've  seen suggestions you're gonna get 15  million i believe it when i see it  but generally speaking it's not you're  not doing the player  a favor and i guarantee you that kenny  gallaudet if he hits the open market  is going to sign a contract with fully  guaranteed money that dwarfs  whatever the franchise tag ends up being  for receivers this year so i just i just  don't like that because what it does  it it it lies to the fans  and it also gets some in the media who  for whatever reason want to take an  anti-player position it gives them some  raw meat that they can use to try to get  the fans on the side of this notion that  the players are being greedy why do you  want so much isn't it enough  for you to get this nice chunk of change  why do you want so much more i'm over  that i'm sick of that  these guys are the ones putting their  asses on the line every week these guys  are the ones who  are going across that thick white stripe  out of the football field and assuming  the risk of injury  and it makes it even more offensive to  players that a former player  would spout that crap that dan campbell  spouted today  all due respect i'm gonna  back up to to last year when we had  players tag mike you look at two of the  players tagged  bud dupree and dak prescott and i think  prescott i think we both agree he's  still going to get his money he's still  going to get paid but  bud dupree i'm going to be surprised  mike if he doesn't sign a one-year deal  coming off an acl injury i mean this was  bad for him he could have signed a huge  contract  and gotten that generational money and  he's not going to get that now he's  going to have to come back  off that acl injury and prove himself  yet again to get that big contract that  he could have had  a year ago so sometimes it works out for  players sometimes it doesn't work out  for players but i know you want to sign  the long-term deal when you get the  chance to sign the long-term deal you  look at  joe mixon joe mixon ended up signing his  long-term deal  got 12 million a year got hurt right he  wouldn't get that money this year if he  hadn't signed that long-term deal so  i always think if you get that long-term  deal you need to sign it you need to  make sure that you're going to get your  money  and not just play i know that we talk  about betting on yourself and all that  sort of stuff but  there are just too many injuries in this  game normally to bet on yourself  and it's easier for the quarterback to  say i'm going to go year to year  kirk cousins blaze that trail of all  quarterbacks he blazed that trail  for the quarterback position dak  prescott is following that path  trumaine johnson blazed that trail for  defensive backs the same year that kirk  cousins did people forget about trumaine  johnson  he went two tags with the rams hit the  open market got a big contract from the  jets never lived up to it but you know  what  he's laying on a stack of money like the  guy from breaking bad somewhere right  now a big pile of cash and a storage  unit that's  all his and always will be he played the  game  and he won so uh and and you know with  with dak  one of the arguments i hear all the time  is well what's he really worth on the  market what's he worth what's he worth  we don't know what he's worth because  the cowboys keep blocking him from going  to the market  that's the thing the franchise tag is a  tool used by teams  to keep players from maximizing the  return  on their abilities and their sacrifices  i've never liked that i've accepted it  that was part of the the trade-off to  get free agency  but uh the teams love it the teams use  it and 25 years later here we are still  litigating the question of whether or  not it's a good thing for players or not  generally it is a bad thing and i just  think that players who hear any coach  gm owner or anyone tied to a team saying  otherwise  should be upset about that shireen  if you're talking about kenny gallaudet  mike he's 27  played five games in 2020 20 catches 338  yards  two touchdowns but that you look at the  lions they have  all the receiving core are free agents  kenny galladey marvin jones danny  amendola  mohamed sanu they're all free agents so  if you're the lions  and you're in that position do you  franchise kenny gallaudet i think he  will be franchised but would you  franchise it  i probably would not i probably would  not because he walks away as a free  agent you get compensatory draft pick  consideration now the problem is they  may be thinking about spending  money on a lot of different free agents  because if you sign more than you lose  you don't get anything for kenny  gallaudet  that's how the formula works and it's  far more nuanced than that but the more  guys you sign  the less it matters that you had a guy  leave but yeah i don't know if they've  got some twisted logic here where  they're gonna tag and trade him  i i just don't know but but just don't  don't just don't don't tell me it's a  good thing if it happens it's not a good  thing if it happens it's it's  rarely anything you could even remotely  argue  is a good thing for a player it keeps  the player from going to the market and  getting the best  deal that he possibly can  hi i'm mike tarico and thanks for  watching make sure to hit subscribe for  the latest news and highlights  from nbc sports
 

